            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "—Deborah Tannen, The Argument Culture: Stopping America's War of Words",
            "block_bbox": [
                200,
                642,
                771,
                666
            ],
            "block_id": 5,
            "block_order": 4,
            "group_id": 5,
            "global_block_id": 5,
            "global_group_id": 5,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    200.0,
                    642.0
                ],
                [
                    771.0,
                    642.0
                ],
                [
                    771.0,
                    666.0
                ],
                [
                    200.0,
                    666.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "Our society’s approach to argument, notes linguist Deborah Tannen, is based on an assumption that competing positions and ideas must always be in conflict. To transcend it, we need to recognize that these competing positions and ideas can often be reconciled (284).",
            "block_bbox": [
                113,
                683,
                734,
                782
            ],
            "block_id": 6,
            "block_order": 5,
            "group_id": 6,
            "global_block_id": 6,
            "global_group_id": 6,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    113.0,
                    683.0
                ],
                [
                    734.0,
                    683.0
                ],
                [
                    734.0,
                    782.0
                ],
                [
                    113.0,
                    782.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "Our paraphrase doesn't convey the meaning of Tannen's original exactly; it expresses our own understanding of it, which also includes our sense of what aspects of it are most important. Could someone else paraphrase the passage differently? Of course. Even so, we believe our version to be accurate and fair.",
            "block_bbox": [
                78,
                799,
                772,
                902
            ],
            "block_id": 7,
            "block_order": 6,
            "group_id": 7,
            "global_block_id": 7,
            "global_group_id": 7,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    78.0,
                    799.0
                ],
                [
                    772.0,
                    799.0
                ],
                [
                    772.0,
                    902.0
                ],
                [
                    78.0,
                    902.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "When paraphrasing, you can't avoid these kinds of choices, but you can make them deliberately and ethically, which is to say in ways that are faithful to your source, that support your own line of thought, and that serve your readers.",
            "block_bbox": [
                79,
                904,
                772,
                980
            ],
            "block_id": 8,
            "block_order": 7,
            "group_id": 8,
            "global_block_id": 8,
            "global_group_id": 8,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
